From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eduardo Fockink <54868856+EduardoFockink@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 18:50:42 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] drm: AQ_MGPU_CPU_COPY: wait for the GPU asleep before
 reading back

glReadPixels has to wait until the GPU has finished the client's frame
(and the 10-bit repack). The NVIDIA driver busy-waits for that, so the
whole GPU time of each frame was spent spinning a CPU core.

waitForGpuAsleep() creates a native fence, flushes, and poll()s its fd,
so the wait happens asleep in the kernel and glReadPixels finds the work
done. If no fence can be made it returns at once and glReadPixels waits
as before. AQ_MGPU_CPU_COPY_SPIN=1 restores the old behaviour.

Standalone benchmark (GPU kept busy ~10 ms before a 3840x1620 readback):
CPU per frame 12.6 ms -> 5.2 ms. In Hyprland with a YouTube video on the
5120x2160 HDR output at 48 Hz: windowed 52.7% -> 43.6% of a core,
fullscreen 61.8% -> 59.2%. The spin loop disappears from perf; what
remains (~80% of samples) is the driver's memcpy reading the readback
staging memory, which is the cost of the CPU copy itself.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
